== 6. FINDING D (advisory, measured) — the phantom opera_state row ==
packages/commands/src/delegate.ts:199 emits workflow.actor_assigned with ITEM_ID = a SELLA id
and ACTOR_ROLE = a MODEL name. Measured end to end on a temp copy of examples/sample-studio:
  $ bisellium delegate --sella builder-1 --model gpt-5.6-sol --studio <tmp>
  .bisellium/events.jsonl:
    {"id":"cli:0","name":"workflow.actor_assigned","attrs":{"workflow.item.id":"builder-1",
     "workflow.actor.role":"gpt-5.6-sol",...}}
  after `query --from-index` built the index, SELECT * FROM opera_state:
    {"id":"builder-1","state":"","collegium":null,"sella":"gpt-5.6-sol","probationes":"{}", ...}
  Index.stats()      -> opera: 8   (seven real opera + the ghost)
  Index.opera()      -> W-001..W-007, builder-1
  Index.opera({state:""}) -> the ghost alone
  Index.timeline("gpt-5.6-sol") -> returns the delegate event (a MODEL behaves as a sella)
  Index.timeline("builder-1")   -> does NOT contain its own delegate event
TWO consumers, not one: packages/core/src/index-db.ts:368 (SQLite) and packages/core/src/store.ts:309
(replay()). The builder's disclosure named core/differ.ts, which PRODUCES actor_assigned; it does
not consume it.
Blast radius today: NOT user-visible. apps/server/src/store.ts's .api.opera re-reads opera/*.md
(measured: GET /api/opera returned 7, not 8), and core/query.ts's statusAnswer is only reachable
for a W-<id>. So this is silent corruption of the derived index with no shipped surface — but it
breaks opera_state's one-row-per-opus invariant and nothing tests it.
Severity: advisory, NOT blocking — shipped under the spec's literal instruction, and a new
workflow.* name is a stop-and-ask (CLAUDE.md / brief 'Out of scope'). Lowest-cost close: drop the
emit for the --sella shape (delegate.ts:198-200). The brief already exempts the --munus shape as
'manifest configuration, not an item lifecycle transition' — a seat's model is the same thing.

